\pard\sa120\sb120\fs28\b Generated Audit Logs (JSON)\b0{\*\bkmkstart generated-audit-logs-json}{\*\bkmkend generated-audit-logs-json}\par
\pard\sa80\fs22 During both normal operations and unit testing (e.g. {\f1 make test\f0}), you will notice a significant number of structured {\f1 .json\f0} log files generated in the {\f1 generated/logs/\f0} subdirectories (such as {\f1 generated/logs/testing/enable_UNIT-TEST-CLUSTER_...json\f0}).\par
\pard\sa80\fs22 These files are the definitive, machine-readable execution records generated by the {\f1 AuditLogger\f0}. They are designed to be ingested programmatically by external monitoring and compliance systems (like OpsRamp and ServiceNow) to confirm state changes, durations, and metadata without parsing plain text logs.\par
\pard\sa80\fs22 \b Important Note on Testing:\b0 During test execution, these JSON logs are intentionally written to {\f1 generated/logs/testing/\f0} to validate that the audit mechanics work end-to-end without polluting the production audit trails. \b Do not change their filenames or suppress their generation\b0 in the test runner, as doing so breaks the execution record structure required for GDPR compliance and external system integrations. A log pruning script ({\f1 scripts/prune-logs.ps1\f0}) automatically manages retention to prevent these from bloating the repository.\par
